  19:./src/stm32f7xx_hal_iwdg.c ****   * If no LICENSE file comes with this software, it is provided AS-IS.
  20:./src/stm32f7xx_hal_iwdg.c ****   *
  21:./src/stm32f7xx_hal_iwdg.c ****   ******************************************************************************
  22:./src/stm32f7xx_hal_iwdg.c ****   @verbatim
  23:./src/stm32f7xx_hal_iwdg.c ****   ==============================================================================
  24:./src/stm32f7xx_hal_iwdg.c ****                     ##### IWDG Generic features #####
  25:./src/stm32f7xx_hal_iwdg.c ****   ==============================================================================
  26:./src/stm32f7xx_hal_iwdg.c ****   [..]
  27:./src/stm32f7xx_hal_iwdg.c ****     (+) The IWDG can be started by either software or hardware (configurable
  28:./src/stm32f7xx_hal_iwdg.c ****         through option byte).
  29:./src/stm32f7xx_hal_iwdg.c **** 
  30:./src/stm32f7xx_hal_iwdg.c ****     (+) The IWDG is clocked by the Low-Speed Internal clock (LSI) and thus stays
  31:./src/stm32f7xx_hal_iwdg.c ****         active even if the main clock fails.
  32:./src/stm32f7xx_hal_iwdg.c **** 
  33:./src/stm32f7xx_hal_iwdg.c ****     (+) Once the IWDG is started, the LSI is forced ON and both cannot be
  34:./src/stm32f7xx_hal_iwdg.c ****         disabled. The counter starts counting down from the reset value (0xFFF).
  35:./src/stm32f7xx_hal_iwdg.c ****         When it reaches the end of count value (0x000) a reset signal is
  36:./src/stm32f7xx_hal_iwdg.c ****         generated (IWDG reset).
  37:./src/stm32f7xx_hal_iwdg.c **** 
  38:./src/stm32f7xx_hal_iwdg.c ****     (+) Whenever the key value 0x0000 AAAA is written in the IWDG_KR register,
  39:./src/stm32f7xx_hal_iwdg.c ****         the IWDG_RLR value is reloaded into the counter and the watchdog reset
  40:./src/stm32f7xx_hal_iwdg.c ****         is prevented.
  41:./src/stm32f7xx_hal_iwdg.c **** 
  42:./src/stm32f7xx_hal_iwdg.c ****     (+) The IWDG is implemented in the VDD voltage domain that is still functional
  43:./src/stm32f7xx_hal_iwdg.c ****         in STOP and STANDBY mode (IWDG reset can wake up the CPU from STANDBY).
  44:./src/stm32f7xx_hal_iwdg.c ****         IWDGRST flag in RCC_CSR register can be used to inform when an IWDG
  45:./src/stm32f7xx_hal_iwdg.c ****         reset occurs.
  46:./src/stm32f7xx_hal_iwdg.c **** 
  47:./src/stm32f7xx_hal_iwdg.c ****     (+) Debug mode: When the microcontroller enters debug mode (core halted),
  48:./src/stm32f7xx_hal_iwdg.c ****         the IWDG counter either continues to work normally or stops, depending
  49:./src/stm32f7xx_hal_iwdg.c ****         on DBG_IWDG_STOP configuration bit in DBG module, accessible through
  50:./src/stm32f7xx_hal_iwdg.c ****         __HAL_DBGMCU_FREEZE_IWDG() and __HAL_DBGMCU_UNFREEZE_IWDG() macros.
  51:./src/stm32f7xx_hal_iwdg.c **** 
  52:./src/stm32f7xx_hal_iwdg.c ****     [..] Min-max timeout value @32KHz (LSI): ~125us / ~32.7s
  53:./src/stm32f7xx_hal_iwdg.c ****          The IWDG timeout may vary due to LSI clock frequency dispersion.
  54:./src/stm32f7xx_hal_iwdg.c ****          STM32F7xx devices provide the capability to measure the LSI clock
  55:./src/stm32f7xx_hal_iwdg.c ****          frequency (LSI clock is internally connected to TIM16 CH1 input capture).
  56:./src/stm32f7xx_hal_iwdg.c ****          The measured value can be used to have an IWDG timeout with an
  57:./src/stm32f7xx_hal_iwdg.c ****          acceptable accuracy.
  58:./src/stm32f7xx_hal_iwdg.c **** 
  59:./src/stm32f7xx_hal_iwdg.c ****     [..] Default timeout value (necessary for IWDG_SR status register update):
  60:./src/stm32f7xx_hal_iwdg.c ****          Constant LSI_VALUE is defined based on the nominal LSI clock frequency.
  61:./src/stm32f7xx_hal_iwdg.c ****          This frequency being subject to variations as mentioned above, the
  62:./src/stm32f7xx_hal_iwdg.c ****          default timeout value (defined through constant HAL_IWDG_DEFAULT_TIMEOUT
  63:./src/stm32f7xx_hal_iwdg.c ****          below) may become too short or too long.
  64:./src/stm32f7xx_hal_iwdg.c ****          In such cases, this default timeout value can be tuned by redefining
  65:./src/stm32f7xx_hal_iwdg.c ****          the constant LSI_VALUE at user-application level (based, for instance,
  66:./src/stm32f7xx_hal_iwdg.c ****          on the measured LSI clock frequency as explained above).
  67:./src/stm32f7xx_hal_iwdg.c **** 
  68:./src/stm32f7xx_hal_iwdg.c ****                      ##### How to use this driver #####
  69:./src/stm32f7xx_hal_iwdg.c ****   ==============================================================================
  70:./src/stm32f7xx_hal_iwdg.c ****   [..]
  71:./src/stm32f7xx_hal_iwdg.c ****     (#) Use IWDG using HAL_IWDG_Init() function to :
  72:./src/stm32f7xx_hal_iwdg.c ****       (++) Enable instance by writing Start keyword in IWDG_KEY register. LSI
  73:./src/stm32f7xx_hal_iwdg.c ****            clock is forced ON and IWDG counter starts counting down.
  74:./src/stm32f7xx_hal_iwdg.c ****       (++) Enable write access to configuration registers:
  75:./src/stm32f7xx_hal_iwdg.c ****           IWDG_PR, IWDG_RLR and IWDG_WINR.
  76:./src/stm32f7xx_hal_iwdg.c ****       (++) Configure the IWDG prescaler and counter reload value. This reload
  77:./src/stm32f7xx_hal_iwdg.c ****            value will be loaded in the IWDG counter each time the watchdog is
  78:./src/stm32f7xx_hal_iwdg.c ****            reloaded, then the IWDG will start counting down from this value.
  79:./src/stm32f7xx_hal_iwdg.c ****       (++) Depending on window parameter:
  80:./src/stm32f7xx_hal_iwdg.c ****         (+++) If Window Init parameter is same as Window register value,
  81:./src/stm32f7xx_hal_iwdg.c ****              nothing more is done but reload counter value in order to exit
  82:./src/stm32f7xx_hal_iwdg.c ****              function with exact time base.
  83:./src/stm32f7xx_hal_iwdg.c ****         (+++) Else modify Window register. This will automatically reload
  84:./src/stm32f7xx_hal_iwdg.c ****              watchdog counter.
  85:./src/stm32f7xx_hal_iwdg.c ****       (++) Wait for status flags to be reset.
  86:./src/stm32f7xx_hal_iwdg.c **** 
  87:./src/stm32f7xx_hal_iwdg.c ****     (#) Then the application program must refresh the IWDG counter at regular
  88:./src/stm32f7xx_hal_iwdg.c ****         intervals during normal operation to prevent an MCU reset, using
  89:./src/stm32f7xx_hal_iwdg.c ****         HAL_IWDG_Refresh() function.
  90:./src/stm32f7xx_hal_iwdg.c **** 
  91:./src/stm32f7xx_hal_iwdg.c ****      *** IWDG HAL driver macros list ***
  92:./src/stm32f7xx_hal_iwdg.c ****      ====================================
  93:./src/stm32f7xx_hal_iwdg.c ****      [..]
  94:./src/stm32f7xx_hal_iwdg.c ****        Below the list of most used macros in IWDG HAL driver:
  95:./src/stm32f7xx_hal_iwdg.c ****       (+) __HAL_IWDG_START: Enable the IWDG peripheral
  96:./src/stm32f7xx_hal_iwdg.c ****       (+) __HAL_IWDG_RELOAD_COUNTER: Reloads IWDG counter with value defined in
  97:./src/stm32f7xx_hal_iwdg.c ****           the reload register
  98:./src/stm32f7xx_hal_iwdg.c **** 
  99:./src/stm32f7xx_hal_iwdg.c ****   @endverbatim
 100:./src/stm32f7xx_hal_iwdg.c ****   */
 101:./src/stm32f7xx_hal_iwdg.c **** 
